---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what-we-store', label: 'What we store' },
  { id: 'third-parties', label: 'Third-party services' },
  { id: 'analytics', label: 'Analytics' },
  { id: 'your-choices', label: 'Your choices' },
  { id: 'changes', label: 'Changes' },
  { id: 'contact', label: 'Contact' },
];
const mail = `mailto:${site.email}`;
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="What the Own Your eCommerce website stores in your browser, and how to control it."
  updated="2026-09-10"
  toc={toc}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>The short version:</strong> this site doesn't use advertising cookies or cross-site trackers,
      and it doesn't run analytics. It saves two small preferences in your browser so it can remember
      choices you make. That's all.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-we-store">What we store on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Instead of cookies, the site uses your browser's local storage, which works in a similar way but is
    never sent to our servers. Each item is saved only when you make a choice, and it's used only to
    remember that choice.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Name</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Kept until</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>theme</code></td>
          <td>Remembers whether you picked the light or dark theme.</td>
          <td>You clear your browser data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>cookies-allowed</code></td>
          <td>Remembers your choice in the cookie preferences panel.</td>
          <td>You clear your browser data</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="third-parties">Third-party services</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Fonts.</strong> The site's fonts load from Google's servers (fonts.googleapis.com and
      fonts.gstatic.com). Google receives your IP address and browser details when they load but doesn't use
      cookies for this. See the <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Formspree.</strong> If you send the contact form, your message goes to Formspree, which
      delivers it to us. Nothing is loaded from Formspree until you press send.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We don't use analytics tools today. If we add them, they'll stay off unless you switch them on in the
    cookie preferences panel, we'll respect Global Privacy Control signals, and we'll update this policy
    first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-choices">Your choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the cookie button in the bottom-right corner of any page to review your preferences.</li>
    <li>
      Clear the saved items at any time in your browser settings, usually under "Clear browsing data" or
      "Site data". The site will go back to its defaults.
    </li>
    <li>
      Block cookies and local storage in your browser entirely if you prefer. The site will still work; it
      just won't remember your theme.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
  <p>If we change what the site stores, we'll update this page and the "Last updated" date at the top.</p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about cookies or privacy? Email <a href={mail}>{site.email}</a>, or read our
    <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
